'''Sakami Jinja''' (酒見神社) is a {{ill|Shinto shrine|de|Shinto-Schrein|ja|神社|zh|神社}} located in {{ill|Ichinomiya|de|Ichinomiya|ja|一宮市|zh|一宫市}}, {{ill|Aichi Prefecture|de|Präfektur Aichi|ja|愛知県|zh|爱知县}}. It is a {{ill|Shikinai-sha|de|Shikinai-sha|ja|式内社|1=Shikinaisha|2=ja|3=式内社|qq=draft|zh|式内社|1=Shikinaisha|2=ja|3=式内社|qq=draft}} and its former rank was {{ill|Gōsha|de|Gōsha|ja|郷社|zh|郷社}}. It stands on the site of the Nakashima-no-Miya where {{ill|Princess Yamatohime|de|Prinzessin Yamatohime|ja|倭姫命|zh|倭姬命}} once stayed, and is regarded as one of the original {{ill|Moto Ise Shrines|de|Moto-Ise-Schreine|ja|元伊勢|zh|元伊势}}.

In addition to {{ill|Amaterasu Ōmikami|de|Amaterasu Ōmikami|ja|天照皇大御神|zh|天照皇大御神}}, the shrine also enshrines {{ill|Sakami-zuo-no-Ōgami|de|Sakami-zuo-no-Ōgami|ja|酒弥豆男神|zh|酒弥豆男神}}, the ancestor of sake brewers, and {{ill|Sakami-zuome-no-Okami|de|Sakami-zuome-no-Okami|ja|酒弥豆女神|zh|酒弥豆女神}}. Behind the main hall is the Yamatohime Shrine, dedicated to {{ill|Princess Yamatohime|de|Prinzessin Yamatohime|ja|倭姫命|zh|倭姬命}}.

== History ==
When Princess Yamatohime, seeking a site to enshrine the spirit of Amaterasu Ōmikami, traveled to this area in the 14th year of {{ill|Emperor Suinin|de|Kaiser Suinin|ja|垂仁天皇|zh|垂仁天皇}} from the Ikuragawa-no-Miya in Mino Province (now {{ill|Mizuho|de|Mizuho|ja|瑞穂市|zh|瑞穗市}}), she stayed at what was then known as Kōbe in Owari and enshrined the sacred object on Mount Miya. The shrine was later built by the local people. According to tradition, the original building had round pillars, a thatched roof, and an open interior.

In the 3rd year of the {{ill|Shōhei era|de|Shōhei-Ära|ja|斉衡|zh|斉衡}} (856), by imperial order of {{ill|Emperor Montoku|de|Kaiser Montoku|ja|文徳天皇|zh|文徳天皇}}, two sake brewers—Ōmura no Haji and Komura no Haji—were dispatched from the {{ill|Ise Grand Shrine|de|Großer Schrein von Ise|ja|伊勢神宮|zh|伊势神宫}} to produce the sacred sake offerings. This event marks the site as the birthplace of sake brewing, from which the shrine’s name “Sakami” is derived.

== Grounds ==
The shrine buildings face north, so worshippers pray facing south toward the {{ill|Ise Grand Shrine|de|Großer Schrein von Ise|ja|伊勢神宮|zh|伊势神宫}}. Behind the main hall is the Yamatohime Shrine, featuring an open-walled design.

A large jar (dated to the Kamakura period) said to have stored {{ill|kurozake|de|Schwarzsake|ja|黒酒|zh|黑酒}} and {{ill|shirozake|de|Weißsake|ja|白酒|zh|白酒}} stands beside the hall, and in front lies a stone trough once used in sake production.

== Local Legends ==
Two unusual local legends surround this shrine. First, because the shrine faces north, it was nicknamed “Reverse Sakami Jinja” (逆見神社) after World War II. Second, the address Imaisechō Motokōbe suggests rivalry with Ise, leading to the tale that the shrine was established in competition with the Ise Grand Shrine.

== Access ==
* 20-minute walk (about 1.5 km) from {{ill|Owari Ichinomiya Station|de|Bahnhof Owari-Ichinomiya|ja|尾張一宮駅|zh|尾张一宫站}} on the {{ill|Tōkaidō Main Line|de|Tōkaidō-Hauptlinie|ja|東海道本線|zh|东海道本线}} and {{ill|Ichinomiya Station|de|Bahnhof Ichinomiya|ja|名鉄一宮駅|zh|名铁一宫站}} on the {{ill|Meitetsu Nagoya Main Line|de|Meitetsu-Nagoya-Hauptlinie|ja|名古屋鉄道 名鉄名古屋本線|zh|名古屋铁道 名鉄名古屋本线}} and the {{ill|Bisai Line|de|Bisai-Linie|ja|名鉄尾西線|zh|名鉄尾西线}}.
* {{ill|i-Bus|de|i-Bus|ja|i-バス|zh|i公交}} circular bus to the Sakami Jinja-mae stop.
* Immediately at the Sakami Jinja-mae intersection on {{ill|Aichi Prefectural Route 190|de|Aichi-Präfekturstraße 190|ja|愛知県道190号名古屋一宮線|zh|爱知县道190号名古宮线}}.

